Z||000000||fiero56||07-02-2001||08:10 AM||||I need a positive answer on weather or not the factory '88 2.8 crank has lead-in grooves or not. <P>Anyone know for sure?||||reg||5
Z||000001||Standard||07-02-2001||03:26 PM||||No, it shouldn't.  The factory changed the drilling in the crankshaft in 87 to allow more oil to flow to the connecting rod bearings and lead-in grooves in the crank were no longer necessary.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.webjolt.f2s.com/pictures/wacko.gif">  <BR>1988 Quad 4 Coupe 5 speed (soon!!)<BR>1986 GT 5 speed, 1984 SC Auto<P>AOL: LastMinuteBastrd  AIM: SavedbyJebus||||reg||1
Z||000002||fiero56||07-02-2001||03:56 PM||||I have found the same answer, but it spefically mentions FWD applications, and doesn't mention RWD. <P>My '86 2.8 crank doesn't have the grooves.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Galen||07-02-2001||05:33 PM||||Fiero's are FWD  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.webjolt.f2s.com/pictures/kid.gif">         <BR>1988 Hawaiin Orchid Coupe  <BR>AIM: GalenRPG||||reg||1
Z||000004||PJ||07-03-2001||10:18 AM||||I have an '88 Fiero 2.8 crank on the bench right now and it does have the lead in grooves. FWIW I have a '96 3.1 crank next to it. It isn't grooved and is drilled slightly different than the '88, changing the timing of oil to some of the rod bearings.<P>PJ<BR>ITA #66 Black<BR>||||reg||1
